Title: The Call of the Wild

Credit: Written by

Author: Jack London

Source: Adapted from book

Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

INT. JUDGE MILLER'S HOUSE - DAY

A grand house in the sun-kissed Santa Clara Valley.

BUCK, a large St. Bernard-Scotch shepherd mix, lounges on the cool veranda, surveying his domain.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
Buck did not read the newspapers, or he would have known that trouble was brewing...

Buck watches as children play in the yard, his pride evident.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
...for every tide-water dog, strong of muscle and with warm, long hair, from Puget Sound to San Diego.

EXT. JUDGE MILLER'S ESTATE - DAY

Wide lawns and tall poplars surround the estate. Buck runs freely, playing with the Judge's children, MOLLIE and ALICE.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
He was king over all creeping, crawling, flying things of Judge Miller's place, humans included.

Buck chases a ball thrown by the boys, his muscles rippling with strength.

INT. JUDGE MILLER'S HOUSE - NIGHT

The family gathers around a roaring fire in the library. Buck lies at the Judge's feet, content.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
But Buck did not know that Manuel, one of the gardener's helpers, was an undesirable acquaintance.

EXT. ORCHARD - NIGHT

Buck strolls through the orchard with MANUEL, unaware of the impending danger.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
Manuel had one besetting sin. He loved to play Chinese lottery...

Suddenly, Manuel grabs Buck and ties a rope around his neck.

BUCK  
(Growls)  

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
But when the ends of the rope were placed in the stranger's hands, he growled menacingly.

EXT. COLLEGE PARK FLAG STATION - NIGHT

A solitary MAN waits as Manuel hands over Buck.

MAN  
You might wrap up the goods before you deliver 'm.

Manuel tightens the rope, and Buck struggles, panic rising.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
Never in all his life had he been so vilely treated, and never in all his life had he been so angry.

INT. BAGGAGE CAR - NIGHT

Buck is thrown into a cramped crate, panting heavily.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
The next he knew, he was dimly aware that his tongue was hurting...

Buck growls as a MAN approaches, but he is too weak to fight back.

MAN  
Yep, has fits.

EXT. SALOON BACK SHED - NIGHT

The MAN speaks to a SALOON-KEEPER, showing off his injured hand.

SALON-KEEPER  
How much did the other mug get?

MAN  
A hundred.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
Dazed, suffering intolerable pain from throat and tongue, Buck attempted to face his tormentors.

INT. CAGE - NIGHT

Buck lies in the crate, nursing his wounds.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
He could not understand what it all meant. What did they want with him?

EXT. BACK YARD - DAY

Four MEN carry Buck's crate into a high-walled yard.

A stout MAN in a red sweater approaches with a hatchet.

MAN IN RED SWEATER  
Now, you red-eyed devil...

Buck surges against the bars, snarling.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
And Buck was truly a red-eyed devil, as he drew himself together for the spring...

INT. BACK YARD - DAY

The MAN swings the hatchet, creating an opening. Buck lunges forward but is struck down by the club.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
He had never been struck by a club in his life, and did not understand.

Buck collapses, bloodied and dazed.

MAN IN RED SWEATER  
You've learned your place, and I know mine.

Buck endures the man's touch, his pride wounded but not broken.

EXT. NARWHAL DECK - DAY

Buck and CURLY, a friendly Newfoundland, are led away by PERREAULT and FRANCOIS.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
Curly and he were taken below by Perrault and turned over to a black-faced giant called Francois.

INT. NARWHAL - DAY

Buck and Curly meet DAVE, a gloomy dog, and SPITZ, a cunning leader.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
He was a new kind of man to Buck, and while he developed no affection for them, he grew honestly to respect them.

EXT. NARWHAL DECK - DAY

Buck steps onto the cold deck, snow falling around him.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
He felt it, as did the other dogs, and knew that a change was at hand.

Buck sniffs the snow, puzzled by its cold bite.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
This was his first snow.

EXT. DYE BEACH - DAY

Buck stands on the beach, surrounded by chaos.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
Buck's first day on the Dyea beach was like a nightmare...

He watches as CURLEY is attacked by a pack of savage dogs.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
It is true, it was a vicarious experience, else he would not have lived to profit by it.

Buck witnesses the brutal fight, his eyes wide with shock.

EXT. DYE BEACH - LATER

Francois straps a harness onto Buck, forcing him into work.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
Though his dignity was sorely hurt by thus being made a draught animal, he was too wise to rebel.

Buck pulls a sled, learning quickly under the stern guidance of Francois and the other dogs.

EXT. DYE BEACH - NIGHT

Buck digs a hole in the snow, exhausted but determined.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
He slept soundly and comfortably, though he growled and barked and wrestled with bad dreams.

EXT. DYE BEACH - DAY

Buck wakes up buried in snow, panic rising as he digs himself out.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
The muscles of his whole body contracted spasmodically and instinctively...

He emerges into the bright daylight, shaking off the snow.

FRANCOIS  
Wot I say? Dat Buck for sure learn queek as anyt'ing.

EXT. DYE BEACH - DAY

Buck is harnessed with his mates, ready to hit the trail.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
He was glad to be gone, and though the work was hard he found he did not particularly despise it.

The team sets off, Buck feeling the thrill of the journey ahead.

EXT. LAKE LE BARGE - NIGHT

A bleak and miserable camp is set up on the shore of Lake Le Barge. Snow falls heavily, and the wind howls like a wild beast.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
The dominant primordial beast was strong in Buck, and under the fierce conditions of trail life it grew and grew.

Francois and Perrault struggle to make a fire on the ice, their faces grim against the biting cold.

FRANCOIS  
(through chattering teeth)  
Dis place, it no good! We freeze!

Buck curls up under a rock, trying to find warmth.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
His newborn cunning gave him poise and control.

EXT. LAKE LE BARGE - LATER

Buck returns to his nest after eating. He finds SPITZ lying in his spot, a warning snarl escaping his lips.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
Till now Buck had avoided trouble with his enemy, but this was too much.

Buck lunges at Spitz, and they tumble into a fierce struggle.

FRANCOIS  
A-a-ah!  
Gif it to heem, by Gar! Gif it to heem, the dirty t'eef!

The two dogs circle each other, growling, ready to fight.

EXT. LAKE LE BARGE - NIGHT

Suddenly, a pack of starving HUSKIES bursts into the camp, drawn by the scent of food. Chaos erupts as they attack the sled-dogs.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
The camp was suddenly discovered to be alive with skulking furry forms.

Buck fights valiantly against the invaders, blood spraying as he sinks his teeth into a HUSKY's throat.

EXT. LAKE LE BARGE - LATER

The team-dogs gather, wounded and exhausted. Buck stands among them, panting heavily.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
Though unpursued, they were in a sorry plight.

Dolly, a new addition to the team, is badly injured. Buck watches, concern etched on his face.

EXT. TAHKEENA RIVER - NIGHT

The team struggles through the icy terrain, Buck limping painfully. Francois rubs Buck's feet, trying to ease his suffering.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
Francois sacrificed the tops of his own moccasins to make four moccasins for Buck.

Buck looks up, gratitude in his eyes.

EXT. TAHKEENA RIVER - LATER

As they prepare to harness up, DOLLY suddenly goes mad, howling and lunging at Buck.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
He had never seen a dog go mad, nor did he have any reason to fear madness.

Buck bolts, terror in his eyes, as he races away from the crazed dog.

FRANCOIS  
(shouting)  
Buck! Come back!

Francois swings an axe, striking Dolly down just as Buck reaches him.

EXT. TAHKEENA RIVER - NIGHT

Buck, panting and exhausted, leans against the sled. SPITZ seizes the opportunity and attacks, sinking his teeth into Buck's flesh.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
This was Spitz's opportunity.

Francois lashes out at Spitz, who yelps in pain.

FRANCOIS  
One devil, dat Spitz.

EXT. DAWSON - DAY

The team arrives in Dawson, where dogs and men bustle about. Buck observes the chaotic scene, his instincts awakening.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
Here were many men, and countless dogs, and Buck found them all at work.

Buck joins the nocturnal song of the huskies, his voice blending with theirs.

EXT. DAWSON - NIGHT

The aurora borealis lights up the sky as Buck howls with the other dogs, a primal connection stirring within him.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
This song of the huskies might have been the defiance of life.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - DAY

The team sets off again, Buck feeling the thrill of the journey. The snow crunches underfoot as they pull the sled.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
The travel pride had gripped him, and he purposed to make the record trip of the year.

Buck glances back at SPITZ, tension crackling between them.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - NIGHT

The team camps for the night, Buck restless. He senses the growing discord among the dogs.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
The insidious revolt led by Buck had destroyed the solidarity of the team.

Buck watches as SPITZ punishes a fellow dog, a smirk on his face.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - DAY

Buck leads the pack in a chase after a snowshoe rabbit, instincts igniting as he runs.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
All that stirring of old instincts... the joy to kill--all this was Buck's.

Buck races ahead, the thrill of the hunt coursing through him.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - LATER

SPITZ cuts across Buck's path, catching the rabbit mid-leap. Buck charges at him, fury in his eyes.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
The time had come. It was to the death.

The two dogs circle each other, the tension palpable as the pack watches.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - NIGHT

The fight erupts, Buck and SPITZ clashing fiercely, blood flying in the moonlight.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
Buck possessed a quality that made for greatness—imagination.

Buck finally gains the upper hand, overpowering SPITZ, the pack closing in around them.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
He stood and looked on, the successful champion, the dominant primordial beast who had made his kill and found it good.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - MORNING

FRANCOIS kneels by the fire, examining Buck's wounds. He shakes his head in disbelief.

FRANCOIS  
Eh? Wot I say? I spik true w'en I say dat Buck two devils.

PERREAULT approaches, surveying Buck with concern.

PERREAULT  
Dat Spitz fight lak hell.

FRANCOIS  
An' dat Buck fight lak two hells. An' now we make good time. No more Spitz, no more trouble, sure.

Francois packs the camp outfit while Perrault loads the sled. Buck trots up to the spot where Spitz would have led.

FRANCOIS  
Eh? Look at dat Buck. Heem keel dat Spitz, heem t'ink to take de job.

Buck lunges at SOL-LEKS, asserting his dominance.

FRANCOIS  
Go 'way, Chook!

SOL-LEKS backs away, intimidated.

FRANCOIS  
Now, by Gar, I feex you!

Francois grabs a heavy club, but Buck circles him, snarling.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
Buck was in open revolt. He wanted, not to escape a clubbing, but to have the leadership.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - LATER

Francois and Perrault chase Buck around the camp, throwing clubs and cursing.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
They cursed him, and his fathers and mothers before him, and all his seed to come after him down to the remotest generation.

Buck dodges their attempts, snarling defiantly.

FRANCOIS  
Time is flying, and we should have been on the trail an hour gone.

Francois scratches his head, looking at Perrault, who shrugs.

FRANCOIS  
Now, I feex you!

Francois unfastens Sol-leks and calls to Buck.

FRANCOIS  
T'row down de club!

Perrault commands, and Francois complies. Buck trots in, triumphant, taking his place at the front of the team.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - DAY

The team races forward, Buck leading with newfound authority.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
Highly as the dog-driver had forevalued Buck, he found that he had undervalued.

Buck excels in leadership, showing quick judgment and action.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
But it was in giving the law and making his mates live up to it, that Buck excelled.

Buck punishes PIKE for loafing, shaking him until he pulls more weight.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - NIGHT

The team makes camp. Buck lies near the fire, exhausted but proud.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
Best of all, perhaps, he loved to lie near the fire, hind legs crouched under him, fore legs stretched out in front.

Buck's thoughts drift back to his past, memories of Judge Miller's house fading.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
Far more potent were the memories of his heredity that gave things he had never seen before a seeming familiarity.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - LATER

Buck watches the flickering flames, his eyes blinking dreamily.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
It seemed that the flames were of another fire, and that as he crouched there he saw another and different man.

The vision of a primitive man appears in Buck's mind, evoking a sense of connection to his ancestry.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
And dreaming there by the Yukon bank, these sounds and sights of another world would make the hair to rise along his back.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - DAY

The team continues their journey, but the weight of the mail slows them down. Buck maintains discipline among the dogs.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
It was a monotonous life, operating with machine-like regularity.

Buck pushes his mates to work harder, despite their exhaustion.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - NIGHT

The camp is set. Buck watches as the drivers attend to the dogs first, ensuring they eat before themselves.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
Each night the dogs were attended to first. They ate before the drivers ate.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - DAY

The team struggles through the snow, Buck leading them with determination.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
But Buck stood it, keeping his mates up to their work and maintaining discipline, though he, too, was very tired.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - LATER

The team arrives at Cassiar Bar. DAVE, visibly weak, is taken out of the team.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
Sick as he was, Dave resented being taken out, grunting and growling.

DAVE struggles against the half-breed's attempts to remove him from the traces.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - DAY

The sled starts moving again, but DAVE flounders alongside, desperate to return to his place.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
He pleaded with his eyes to remain there.

DAVE bites through SOL-LEKS's traces, determined to reclaim his position.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - NIGHT

The camp is quiet. A revolver shot rings out, breaking the stillness.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
But Buck knew, and every dog knew, what had taken place behind the belt of river trees.

EXT. WHITE RIVER CAMP - DAY

Buck lies on the ground, exhausted and bruised. JOHN THORNTON kneels beside him, examining his injuries.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
Buck had suffered greatly, but he was not broken.

JOHN THORNTON  
(softly)  
You’ve been through hell, haven’t you, boy?

Buck looks up at JOHN, sensing kindness.

EXT. WHITE RIVER CAMP - LATER

JOHN THORNTON sets down a bowl of water in front of Buck. Buck drinks eagerly, his strength slowly returning.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
In the presence of this man, Buck felt a flicker of hope.

JOHN THORNTON  
You’ll be alright now. No more whips, no more chains.

Buck finishes drinking and looks at JOHN with gratitude.

EXT. WHITE RIVER CAMP - DAY

JOHN THORNTON stands, surveying the camp. He notices the remnants of the sled and the chaos left behind.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
The remnants of the past were scattered, but a new journey awaited.

JOHN THORNTON  
(to himself)  
Time to start fresh.

EXT. WHITE RIVER CAMP - DAY

Buck watches as JOHN begins to gather supplies. He feels a new sense of purpose.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
Buck was ready to embrace the wild once more.

EXT. WHITE RIVER CAMP - DAY

JOHN THORNTON approaches Buck, holding a piece of dried meat.

JOHN THORNTON  
Here you go, boy. You need to eat.

Buck takes the meat eagerly, his spirit rekindled.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
With each bite, Buck regained his strength and his will to survive.

EXT. WHITE RIVER CAMP - DAY

JOHN THORNTON and Buck sit together by the fire. The sun sets, casting a warm glow over the camp.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
In the heart of the wilderness, Buck found a new home.

JOHN THORNTON  
You’re a good dog, Buck. We’re going to make it together.

Buck leans against JOHN, feeling safe for the first time in a long while.

EXT. WHITE RIVER CAMP - NIGHT

The stars twinkle above as Buck lies beside JOHN, who is asleep by the fire. Buck stirs, sensing the call of the wild.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
The wild was calling him, whispering promises of freedom and adventure.

Buck stands, looking into the dark woods, a spark of wildness igniting within him.

EXT. WOODS - NIGHT

Buck runs into the woods, the moonlight illuminating his path. He feels the pulse of the wild around him.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
He was no longer just a pet; he was a creature of the wild.

EXT. WOODS - NIGHT

Buck howls at the moon, a sound of triumph and belonging.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
In that moment, Buck embraced his true nature.

EXT. WHITE RIVER CAMP - NIGHT

JOHN THORNTON stirs awake, hearing Buck's howl. He smiles, knowing Buck is finding his place.

JOHN THORNTON  
(softly)  
Go on, Buck. Find your way.

Buck returns to the camp, a new light in his eyes.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
With each passing day, Buck would grow stronger, more attuned to the wild.

EXT. WHITE RIVER CAMP - DAY

JOHN THORNTON and Buck prepare to leave the camp. Buck stands tall, ready for the journey ahead.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
Together, they would face whatever challenges lay ahead.

JOHN THORNTON  
Let’s go, Buck. Adventure awaits!

They set off into the wilderness, Buck leading the way with newfound strength and purpose.

EXT. WHITE RIVER CAMP - DAY

Buck lies by the riverbank, soaking in the warmth of the sun. JOHN THORNTON sits nearby, watching the water flow.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
As spring unfolded, Buck regained his strength, surrounded by love and companionship.

SKEET, a small Irish setter, approaches Buck, licking his wounds gently. NIG, a large black dog, joins them, wagging his tail.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
For the first time, Buck experienced genuine love, a bond that transcended mere ownership.

JOHN THORNTON  
(to Buck)  
You’re getting stronger every day, my friend.

Buck gazes up at Thornton, his eyes filled with adoration.

EXT. WHITE RIVER CAMP - DAY

Buck plays with SKEET and NIG, engaging in a series of joyful games. JOHN THORNTON laughs, joining in the fun.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
In this newfound existence, Buck found joy in the simplest of moments.

JOHN THORNTON  
Come on, Buck! Show them what you’ve got!

Buck leaps into the air, barking playfully.

EXT. CIRCLE CITY - DAY

The bustling saloon is filled with miners. JOHN THORNTON stands at the bar, surrounded by a group of men boasting about their dogs.

JOHN THORNTON  
Buck can start a thousand pounds.

The crowd murmurs in disbelief. A man, MATTHEWSON, steps forward, challenging Thornton.

MATTHEWSON  
I’ll bet a thousand dollars he can’t.

JOHN THORNTON  
(steadfast)  
And break it out? And walk off with it for a hundred yards?

The tension in the room thickens as Thornton’s confidence wavers.

EXT. CIRCLE CITY - DAY

The crowd gathers outside, eyes fixed on the sled loaded with flour. Buck stands ready, harnessed and eager.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
The moment of truth had arrived, and Buck felt the weight of expectation.

JOHN THORNTON  
(to Buck)  
As you love me, Buck. As you love me.

Buck whines, understanding the gravity of the moment.

JOHN THORNTON  
Now, Buck!

Buck tightens the traces, then lunges forward, straining against the sled.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
With every ounce of strength, Buck pushed against the impossible.

The sled begins to move, slowly at first, then gaining momentum.

EXT. CIRCLE CITY - DAY

The crowd erupts in cheers as Buck pulls the sled, his muscles rippling with effort. JOHN THORNTON runs alongside, encouraging him.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
In that moment, Buck was not just a dog; he was a force of nature.

The sled crosses the finish line, and the crowd goes wild, hats flying in the air.

JOHN THORNTON  
(tearfully)  
You did it, Buck! You did it!

Buck, panting and proud, stands beside Thornton, who embraces him tightly.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
Together, they had forged an unbreakable bond, one that would withstand the trials of the wild.

EXT. CIRCLE CITY - DAY

The crowd disperses, but JOHN THORNTON remains with Buck, their connection stronger than ever.

JOHN THORNTON  
You’re not just my dog, Buck. You’re my heart.

Buck leans against Thornton, content and fulfilled.

EXT. FOREST - NIGHT

The moonlight filters through the trees, casting eerie shadows on the ground. BUCK stands alone, his fur glistening, alert and poised.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
Buck felt the call of the wild echoing through the forest, stronger than ever before.

A faint howl drifts through the air, and BUCK's ears perk up. He looks toward the sound, eyes filled with longing.

EXT. CAMP - NIGHT

JOHN THORNTON sits by the fire, eating dinner. BUCK bursts into the camp, leaping onto THORNTON, overwhelming him with affection.

JOHN THORNTON  
(laughing)  
You crazy dog! What’s gotten into you?

BUCK licks THORNTON's face, his joy palpable.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
For two days and nights, Buck never left Thornton’s side, but the call grew stronger.

EXT. FOREST - NIGHT

BUCK wanders through the woods, restless. He sniffs the air, searching for the wild brother.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
The wild brother was gone, but the call beckoned him deeper into the forest.

EXT. VALLEY - DAY

BUCK moves through a lush valley, hunting and exploring. He catches a glimpse of a timber wolf in the distance.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
He felt the thrill of the hunt, the freedom of the wild, and the pride of survival.

EXT. CAMP - DAY

BUCK returns to the camp, but something feels off. The air is thick with tension. He senses danger.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
But the world was changing, and Buck could feel it in his bones.

EXT. CAMP - DAY

BUCK discovers NIG lying dead, an arrow protruding from his side. He growls, sensing the threat.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
The Yeehats had come, and they brought death with them.

EXT. CAMP - DAY

BUCK races through the camp, finding HANS and PETE, both dead. His heart races with fury.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
In that moment, Buck became a force of nature, driven by love and rage.

EXT. CAMP - DAY

The YEHAATS dance around the wreckage of the lodge. BUCK charges at them, a whirlwind of fury.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
He was no longer just a dog; he was a hurricane of vengeance.

BUCK tears through the YEHAATS, ripping and snarling, a blur of motion. The YEHAATS panic and flee.

EXT. CAMP - DAY

BUCK stands over the fallen YEHAATS, panting heavily. He looks around, the camp now silent.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
But the victory was hollow. The one he loved was gone.

EXT. POOL - DAY

BUCK approaches the muddy pool, where JOHN THORNTON lies beneath the surface. He howls in grief.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
The last tie was broken. Buck was free.

EXT. FOREST - NIGHT

The moon shines brightly as BUCK stands in the clearing, listening to the call. The WOLF PACK emerges from the shadows.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
The call of the wild was irresistible, and Buck answered it.

BUCK howls, joining the pack, running alongside his wild brother.

EXT. VALLEY - NIGHT

The pack races through the moonlit forest, BUCK leading the way, a majestic figure among them.

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
And so, Buck became one with the wild, a ghost among wolves, forever free.

FADE OUT.

THE END.
